<Base title="My homelab" description="The hardware and the ~30 self-hosted services running on my home server, and what each one is for.">
  <div class="prose" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1>My homelab</h1>
    <p class="lede">One quiet box in my room, about thirty Docker containers, zero monthly subscriptions. Here's what's on it and why.</p>

    <h2>Hardware</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">What I use</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>CPU</td><td>AMD Ryzen 7 5700X (8 cores / 16 threads)</td><td>Plenty for game servers and dozens of containers. Idles around 38 °C.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>RAM</td><td>32 GB</td><td>Modded Minecraft alone wants 8–12 GB. Everything else fits comfortably.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>GPU</td><td>GTX 1050 Ti (4 GB)</td><td>Hardware video transcoding for Jellyfin and small local AI models.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>System disk</td><td>500 GB NVMe SSD</td><td>OS, Docker and all app configs. Fast, and only about a quarter full.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Data disk</td><td>2 TB HDD</td><td>Media library, camera recordings and backups.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Extras</td><td>Sonoff Zigbee 3.0 USB dongle</td><td>Talks to wireless sensors for Home Assistant.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The OS is Ubuntu Server 22.04. Every service runs in Docker, one <code>docker compose</code> file per "stack", which makes things easy to back up, move and rebuild.</p>

    <h2>What runs on it</h2>
    <h3>Media</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Jellyfin</strong>: my own Netflix, with GPU transcoding.</li>
      <li><strong>Sonarr, Radarr, Prowlarr, Bazarr, Jellyseerr</strong>: the "*arr" stack that finds, organises and subtitles the library.</li>
      <li><strong>Navidrome</strong>: music streaming, plus a small downloader I wrote that tags files properly.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Network</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>AdGuard Home + Unbound</strong>: ad blocking for every device in the house and my own recursive DNS resolver. <a href="/blog/adguard-unbound-network-adblock/">How I set it up</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Nginx Proxy Manager</strong>: friendly local names with HTTPS for every service.</li>
      <li><strong>Tailscale</strong>: remote access from my phone without opening a single port on the router.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Smart home</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Home Assistant</strong>, <strong>Zigbee2MQTT</strong> and <strong>Mosquitto</strong> for sensors. <a href="/blog/zigbee2mqtt-restart-loop-docker-network/">The time Zigbee2MQTT restarted 22,000 times</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Frigate</strong>: camera recording with object detection.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>AI</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Ollama + Open WebUI + SearXNG</strong>: a private ChatGPT-style assistant with web search, squeezed onto a 4 GB GPU. <a href="/blog/local-ai-ollama-4gb-gpu/">What actually fits</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Game servers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Valheim</strong> dedicated server for my friends. <a href="/blog/valheim-dedicated-server-docker/">Setup guide</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Crafty Controller</strong> for Minecraft, including heavy modpacks like All The Mods 10.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Everything else</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Vaultwarden</strong>: self-hosted password manager (Bitwarden compatible).</li>
      <li><strong>Homarr</strong>: a dashboard with a tile for every service.</li>
      <li><strong>Uptime Kuma</strong> and <strong>Netdata</strong>: monitoring and alerts.</li>
      <li><strong>Portainer</strong>: a web UI for Docker when I'm too lazy for the terminal.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tools I built along the way</h2>
    <p>Small browser tools that came out of running this server: a <a href="/tools/dns-checker/">DNS record checker</a>, a <a href="/tools/compose-port-checker/">Compose port checker</a>, a <a href="/tools/docker-log-limits/">Docker log limit helper</a>, plus helpers for my Valheim and Minecraft servers. <a href="/tools/">All tools</a>.</p>

    <h2>The rules I run it by</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>No port forwards.</strong> Nothing on this server is reachable from the internet. Remote access goes through Tailscale. <a href="/blog/homelab-security-no-port-forwards/">Why</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Nightly backups</strong> of every config. <a href="/blog/homelab-backup-script-docker/">The script</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Everything in Compose files</strong>, so any service can be rebuilt in a minute.</li>
      <li><strong>Write it down when it breaks.</strong> That's what this site is.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>
